// A volatile store may not be elided by a later store to the same location.
//
// MoRDor's own test, for #83. `x :v= r1` is overwritten by `x :v= 7`, so write
// elision would drop it and with it the dependency on the read of y. Volatile
// says every store is a real access to memory, so the elision is not available
// and the dependency stands in every execution.
//
// relaxed-store-elided.lit is the same program without the volatile
// annotations, and asserts the opposite. The pair separates on volatility
// alone, which is the point: `volatile` was parsed, printed with a `v` prefix
// and consulted by no relation at all, so the two files behaved identically.
//
// Volatile now keeps its accesses out of the elided position of both `fwd` and
// `we` in Elaborations.ForwardElab. This file goes from 8 executions to 1.
//
// Non-elision is ALL volatile means here. It derives no ppo, dp, hb or sw edge,
// no model reads the flag, and a volatile access takes part in co and rf just
// as the relaxed one would -- so it still admits every relaxed outcome. This is
// neither Java's volatile (sequentially consistent) nor C/C++'s (a constraint
// on the abstract machine). Ordering is what rel/acq/sc are for.
y :v= 0;
x :v= 0;
r1 :v= y;
x :v= r1;
x :v= 7;
%% forbid ((3,4) ∉ .dp) []
